winewayland: click-through decorative overlays are subsurfaces, not xdg_popups.

SYMPTOM (Lightroom Classic): the shading Lightroom draws over its filmstrip floated above every
other application on the desktop, including non-Wine ones. Click over to a browser and Lightroom's
overlay stayed on top of it.

WS_EX_LAYERED|WS_EX_TRANSPARENT is the same pair this file already uses to give a window an empty
input region -- it cannot be clicked -- and WS_EX_NOACTIVATE says it must never become the active
window. Nothing that can be neither clicked nor activated is a menu; it is chrome the application
paints over its own frame. Such a window must be stacked WITH its parent, so it is a subsurface.

An xdg_popup is a semi-detached surface the compositor stacks above the parent and it stays there.
On Windows WS_EX_TOPMOST really is absolute in the z-order, so the application is right to keep
the overlay up; Wayland has no such concept, so the only honest mapping is to stack it with the
window it decorates.

⚠️ Fixing the deactivation path first PROVED this is not an activation bug -- see the companion
patch. The foreground moves to the desktop as it should and the overlay still did not sink.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -642,20 +642,43 @@
     if (!data && !(data = wayland_win_data_create(hwnd, rects))) return FALSE;
 
     wayland_win_data_release(data);
 
     return TRUE;
 }
 
 /***********************************************************************
  *           WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged
  */
+/* NEUTRON: is this window a click-through decorative overlay rather than a menu?
+ *
+ * WS_EX_LAYERED|WS_EX_TRANSPARENT is the same pair this file already uses to give a window an
+ * empty input region -- it cannot be clicked -- and WS_EX_NOACTIVATE says it must never become
+ * the active window. Nothing that can be neither clicked nor activated is a menu; it is chrome
+ * the application paints over its own frame.
+ *
+ * Such a window must be a SUBSURFACE of its owner, not an xdg_popup. A popup is a semi-detached
+ * surface the compositor stacks above the parent, and it stays there: Lightroom's filmstrip bezel
+ * (1210x796, layered+transparent+topmost, owned by AgWinMainFrame) floated above every other
+ * application on the desktop, including non-Wine ones, and did not sink when Lightroom lost focus.
+ *
+ * ⚠️ Fixing the deactivation path FIRST proved this is not an activation bug: with
+ * WM_WAYLAND_KILL_FOREGROUND in place the foreground demonstrably moves to the desktop when the
+ * user clicks another application (verified with a foreground probe), the handler logs
+ * DEACTIVATING, and the bezel still does not sink -- because on Windows WS_EX_TOPMOST really is
+ * absolute in the z-order, so the application is right to keep it up. Wayland has no such
+ * concept, so the only honest mapping is to stack it with its parent. */
+static BOOL neutron_is_clickthrough_overlay(HWND hwnd)
+{
+    DWORD ex = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE);
+    return (ex & WS_EX_LAYERED) && (ex & WS_EX_TRANSPARENT) && (ex & WS_EX_NOACTIVATE);
+}
 void WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged(HWND hwnd, HWND insert_after, HWND owner_hint, UINT swp_flags,
                               const struct window_rects *new_rects, struct window_surface *surface)
 {
     BOOL needs_expose = FALSE;
     HWND toplevel = NtUserGetAncestor(hwnd, GA_ROOT), owner = NULL;
     struct wayland_surface *toplevel_surface = NULL, *owner_surface = NULL;
     struct wayland_client_surface *client;
     struct wayland_win_data *data, *toplevel_data, *owner_data;
     BOOL managed, fullscreen = swp_flags & WINE_SWP_FULLSCREEN;
 
@@ -740,20 +763,21 @@
      *  - its owner is already a mapped xdg_surface (toplevel or popup),
      *  - it isn't already a subsurface — re-roling a live surface destroys and
      *    recreates it, leaving a ghost frame; and menus reach this on first show
      *    anyway since their owner (the main window) is already mapped,
      *  - the owner relationship isn't circular.
      * Otherwise fall back to the classic behavior: a subsurface of the owner.
      * This keeps a popup candidate whose owner isn't ready yet (e.g. a splash
      * sub-window) as a subsurface of its owner, rather than briefly flashing as
      * a standalone toplevel (which showed as a ghost second window). */
     if (owner_surface && owner_surface->xdg_surface &&
+        !neutron_is_clickthrough_overlay(hwnd) &&
         !(data->wayland_surface &&
           data->wayland_surface->role == WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_SUBSURFACE) &&
         !((owner_surface->role == WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_SUBSURFACE &&
            owner_surface->toplevel_hwnd == hwnd) ||
           (owner_surface->role == WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_POPUP &&
            owner_surface->owner_hwnd == hwnd)))
     {
         /* popup: anchored to its owner, not a subsurface of any toplevel */
         toplevel_data = NULL;
         toplevel_surface = NULL;
